  "tour": {
    "title": "From Jesus’ Empty Tomb to Your Resurrected Body",
    "intro": "This tour follows the New Testament’s sequence rather than treating “resurrection” and “going to heaven” as interchangeable. It starts with what the word resurrection means in the case of Jesus, then traces what the teachers hear Paul saying about the interval after death, the transformation of the body, and the final hope of renewed creation.",
    "steps": [
      {
        "recordId": "ask-nt-wright:podcast:6a253c6-com-651c11dc-e7c9-3e9c-8d28-dfdec7d811d5",
        "startSec": 500,
        "endSec": 694,
        "why": "Begin here to separate the two ideas your question is testing. N. T. Wright explains why the empty tomb together with the appearances points to “a physical resurrection,” then says plainly that “resurrection is not about somebody going into a glorious angelic state” and is “simply not” the same as going to heaven. Jesus’ resurrection supplies the pattern for everything that follows.",
        "episodeTitle": "#164 Easter questions on the resurrection of Jesus",
        "sourceId": "ask-nt-wright",
        "source": "Ask NT Wright Anything",
        "audioUrl": "https://traffic.megaphone.fm/NSR6878813537.mp3?updated=1749655811",
        "durationSec": 2012,
        "adInsertionDrift": true
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:7dbd0c70-com-ad14c96d-7e81-3bdd-a910-a23bb2f5b5f9",
        "startSec": 193,
        "endSec": 480,
        "why": "Now listen for the sequence after death. This commentator acknowledges that 2 Corinthians 5 has “a lack of precise timeline,” but reads it with Paul’s other letters as death, “being with the Lord,” and a later resurrection when Jesus returns. The tent-and-building image is applied directly to “our present physical body” and the future “supernatural and eternal” body.",
        "episodeTitle": "2 Corinthians 5:1-10",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/jckmzx/2_corinthians_51-10816em.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1584,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "bibleproject:podcast:resurrection-way-life-part-4-acorns-fire",
        "startSec": 883,
        "endSec": 1150,
        "why": "This gives the bodily claim its clearest picture. Working through 1 Corinthians 15, the speaker says the body “decays, it gets sick, and it dies,” but “that’s not the end of a human”; the hope is “a new form of physical bodily existence.” Paul’s seed image lets you hear both continuity and transformation, including the contrast between what is “sown perishable” and what is raised imperishable.",
        "episodeTitle": "Acorns of Fire",
        "sourceId": "bibleproject",
        "source": "BibleProject",
        "audioUrl": "https://afp-597195-injected.calisto.simplecastaudio.com/e5d85dd4-a616-4860-846c-fc4d57e11252/episodes/c2774234-47e5-4281-a3bc-72c252ed1861/audio/128/default.mp3?awCollectionId=e5d85dd4-a616-4860-846c-fc4d57e11252&awEpisodeId=c2774234-47e5-4281-a3bc-72c252ed1861",
        "durationSec": 2953,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      },
      {
        "recordId": "listeners-commentary:podcast:b65bd89d-com-e0129c8f-9f93-392f-bcba-0def335d7c00",
        "startSec": 280,
        "endSec": 470,
        "why": "Finish with the New Testament’s final setting. On Revelation 21, the commentator stresses that it is not God’s people going up into heaven, but “the new Jerusalem coming down out of heaven,” restoring the union of heaven and earth. The clip then lingers over God dwelling among his people and the promise that death, mourning, crying, and pain will be gone.",
        "episodeTitle": "Revelation 21:1-22:5",
        "sourceId": "listeners-commentary",
        "source": "The Listener's Commentary",
        "audioUrl": "https://mcdn.podbean.com/mf/web/bwuev4wq3hdtt93e/revelation_211-2259g6v5.mp3",
        "durationSec": 1707,
        "adInsertionDrift": false
      }
    ],
    "closing": "Taken together, these clips present a two-stage hope: death is not the final form of Christian life, and the in-between state is described more briefly and less precisely than the resurrection itself. The final New Testament horizon is not disembodied escape, but God raising and transforming the body and making all things new in a world where God dwells with his people.",
    "totalSeconds": 938
  },
